      "text": "To determine the curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we need to eliminate the parameter \\( t \\).\n\n1. Start with the given parametric equations:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\n   \\]\n\n2. Simplify the expressions for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\n   \\]\n\n3. Isolate \\( \\frac{1}{t} \\) in both equations:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - y\n   \\]\n\n4. Since both expressions are equal to \\( \\frac{1}{t} \\), set them equal to each other:\n   \\[\n   x - 1 = 1 - y\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify to get the equation in terms of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   x - 1 = 1 - y\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - 1 = 1 - y\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - 1 + y = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x + y - 2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. The equation \\( x + y - 2 = 0 \\) is the equation of a line in the \\(xy\\)-plane.\n\nTherefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a line, and the letter of the correct option is \\( \\boxed{\\text{A}} \\).",
