      "text": "Let's solve the given problem step-by-step. We need to find the relationship between the coordinates \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) of the points and determine the type of curve on which all these points lie.\n\n1. First, let's express \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\nSolving for \\(t\\), we get:\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies x - 1 = \\frac{1}{t} \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\]\n\n2. Now, let's express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(t\\):\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\nAgain, solving for \\(t\\), we get:\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\n3. Substituting \\(t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\\) into the equation \\(y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}} = 1 - (x - 1) = 2 - x \\]\n\n4. So, the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is:\n\\[ y = 2 - x \\]\n\nThis equation represents a straight line.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy to ensure our reasoning is accurate.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nt = sp.symbols('t')\nx, y = sp.symbols('x y')\n\n# Express t in terms of x\nt_value = sp.solve(sp.Eq(x, (t + 1) / t), t)[0]\n\n# Substitute t back into the second equation\ny_value = (t_value - 1) / t_value\ny_value = sp.simplify(y_value)\n\n# Display the final equation\nprint(sp.Eq(y, y_value))\n```\n```output\nEq(y, 2 - x)\n```\nThe output confirms our calculations: the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is \\(y = 2 - x\\). This is the equation of a line.\n\nSo, all the plotted points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie on a line. The correct answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\)",
